# Addresses. Change these, restart the app, done. No code to edit.
#
# There are TWO different machines here and they are easy to mix up:

# 1. The SiMa devkit. Answers questions from the operator's manual.
#    8092 is the v4 pipeline; 8090 is v3, still deployed beside it.
#    For one run without editing this file: run.ps1 -Devkit <url>
ASSIST_DEVKIT_URL=http://192.168.94.180:8092

# 2. The simulator. Receives spoken ORDERS -- "fold the boom", "start
#    spraying". Nothing to do with the manual. This is the machine whose IP
#    changes when you move the demo.
ASSIST_TRIGGER_URL=http://192.168.94.40:5000/trigger

#    ...and how an order gets there. `auto` (the default) writes the file
#    directly when that address is this machine, so window_listener.py does
#    not have to be running at all. Force it either way with `local` / `http`.
# ASSIST_TRIGGER_MODE=auto
# ASSIST_COMMAND_FILE=C:/simulator/voice_commands.json

# 3. Chris, the conversational model. llama.cpp on the board, CPU only.
#    Greetings go here; machine questions go to the devkit above.
ASSIST_LLM_URL=http://192.168.94.180:8091/v1/chat/completions

# 4. The simulator's Connections App log. Lessons tick their steps off by
#    reading it -- it is never written to. Only needed when the Connections
#    App lives somewhere other than the path in data/lessons.json.
# ASSIST_SIM_LOG=C:/simulator/Sprayer_VC_v6/net8.0-windows/log.txt

# 5. The welcome kiosk on the SiMa board. It POSTs a sign-in here when an
#    operator taps their ID card, and this app POSTs back when that session
#    ends. Base address only -- /api/logout and /api/session hang off it.
#    The board's IP is DHCP-assigned: if sign-outs stop arriving there, check
#    it with `hostname -I` on the board. Read on every message, so a change
#    here needs no restart.
# ASSIST_KIOSK_URL=http://192.168.94.15:8080

#    ...and the port this app listens on for those sign-ins. It has to match
#    WELCOME_JD_URL in .env ON THE BOARD, which is where the address of THIS
#    machine lives. Windows Firewall prompts for Python on the first sign-in --
#    Allow, or nothing from the board ever arrives.
# ASSIST_LOGIN_PORT=5000
# ASSIST_LOGIN_HOST=0.0.0.0
# ASSIST_KIOSK_TIMEOUT_S=5

# Which panel each window opens on. Run tools/identify_screens.py: it prints
# a token on every monitor, and the token is what goes here. A geometry rather
# than an index, because indices reshuffle when a cable moves or a monitor
# sleeps, and a layout pinned to indices then opens the lesson plan on top of
# the game. Any panel left unlisted is never drawn on -- that is how you keep
# a desktop to drive the demo from.
# ASSIST_SCREEN_CHAT=1920x1080+1920+10
# ASSIST_SCREEN_LESSON=1920x1080-1920+9
# ASSIST_SCREEN_GAME=1920x1080+0+0

# The simulator's own window. It is never launched by this app, only moved --
# left alone it stretches across every monitor, including the two above.
# `max` fills its panel; a WxH is centred on it.
# ASSIST_GAME_TITLE=Farming Simulator
# ASSIST_GAME_SIZE=1600x900

# Who just signed in, written by whatever handles sign-in elsewhere. The name
# is taken from {"name": "..."} , from username/user/operator/displayName, or
# from a bare line of text. Until this file exists the app's own sign-in
# screen is the way in, so nothing waits on that system being ready.
# ASSIST_LOGIN_FILE=C:/simulator/current_user.json

# The trainer's login for the admin screen. Checked in Python; the UI only
# ever learns yes or no.
# ASSIST_ADMIN_USER=admin
# ASSIST_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# The recording a new operator is shown: how to bring the simulator up. It is
# streamed from wherever it lives -- nothing is copied into the app.
# ASSIST_STARTER_VIDEO=C:/Users/LTTS/Videos/Screen Recordings/Screen Recording 2026-05-11 151653.mp4

# Optional.
# ASSIST_RECALL_DELAY_S=2.5  how long a remembered answer takes to come
#                           back. A cache hit is ready in ~6ms, which
#                           reads as though nothing happened. 0 = instant.
# ASSIST_TIMEOUT_S=300     how long to wait for the board
# ASSIST_TRIGGER_TIMEOUT_S=5
# ASSIST_WAKE_THRESHOLD=0.8   3 frames above this fire the wake word.
#                             0.8 is the measured line between
#                             "hey chris" and ordinary speech.
# ASSIST_ECHO_TAIL_S=0.6   how long the wake gate stays shut after the app
#                          finishes speaking, so it cannot hear itself
# ASSIST_BARGE_IN=0        1 keeps the gate live while the app is talking, so
#                          "hey chris" interrupts an answer. Headsets only --
#                          on open speakers it wakes itself.
